# What the build context must not send.
#
# The `Dockerfile` copies the tree, so this file is where what the image must
# not hold is stated, and it is stated as what it removes: a file type nobody
# excluded is a file type that ships.
#
# Named rather than described. "The specification directories" is a phrase two
# readers resolve differently, where `openspec` is a path a test can look for
# inside an image — `checks/container-image-context.test.ts` does exactly that,
# one case per entry below. A path added later is admitted by naming it here,
# which is the decision being taken rather than defaulted.

# The whole history of a public repository, in an image that is world-readable
# and that no run reads a commit out of.
.git

# A developer's own file, holding a STRATZ key and a database password, sitting
# beside this one in the context that is sent to the builder. `.env.example` is
# tracked and therefore in every context on every machine; neither entry point
# reads either.
.env*

# The host's install: another platform's binaries, and every development
# dependency. The production stage performs its own. No trailing separator,
# matching `.gitignore`'s reasoning — a worktree has this as a symlink.
node_modules

# Read by neither entry point: the agent configuration, the specifications, and
# the run outputs of the four tools that write them.
.claude
openspec
test-results
playwright-report
reports
.stryker-tmp

# Built by the build stage and copied from it. A developer's own is stale, and
# `COPY --from` merges into a directory rather than replacing it, so a stale
# bundle sent here would survive beside the fresh one.
dist

# Written by the job at runtime and mounted over at both paths. The server
# answers from its listing of each, so a file shipped in either is a source no
# export can replace.
snapshot
icons
